1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Johannes Linnankoski (originally Vihtori Juhani Peltonen, October 18, 1869 – August 10, 1913) was a Finnish author. His most famous work is the sensual "Laulu tulipunaisesta kukasta". His primary themes were explor
2 CONC ing guilt, punishment and redemption as moral questions.
2 CONT
2 CONT His books have been made into numerous major feature films with the most famous (or notorious) being Hilja maitotyttö (directed by Teuvo Tulio) and Laulu tulipunaisesta kukasta - "The Song of the Blood-Red Flower" - (directed by M
2 CONC ikko Niskanen). "The Song of the Blood-Red Flower" has also been filmed in Sweden, in three different versions by directors Mauritz Stiller, Per-Axel Branner and Gustaf Molander.
2 CONT
2 CONT Linnankoski was also active in the cultural life of Eastern Uusimaa. He was one of the founders of the bank in Porvoo and also founded Finnish-language schools and daily newspapers such as Uusimaa, the first Finnish-language newsp
2 CONC aper situatued outside of the major towns of Uusimaa.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1938 a Linnankoski museum was opened, close to the sauna building where Linnankoski supposedly was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Linnankoski
2 CONT
